\begin{abstract}
We develop a complete and self-contained theory of ellipsoids in $\R^n$ defined as linear
images of the closed Euclidean unit ball, $\E(A) = A B^n$, and derive exact formulas for
the volumes of their central sections in terms of the determinant and the spectrum of the
generating matrix. The central result is the hyperplane section formula
\[
  \vol_{n-1}\bigl(\E(A)\cap u^{\perp}\bigr) = \frac{\lvert\det A\rvert}{\norm{A^\T u}}\,\omega_{n-1},
  \qquad \norm{u} = 1,
\]
where $\omega_m$ denotes the volume of the unit $m$-ball. From it we obtain: two-sided
spectral slicing bounds $\det A/\lambda_{\max} \le \vol_{n-1}(\E(A)\cap u^\perp)/\omega_{n-1}
\le \det A/\lambda_{\min}$ for positive definite generators, together with the exact
equality case---extremal sections occur precisely orthogonal to extremal eigenvectors; the
determinant normalization identity $\prod_i(\det A/\lambda_i) = (\det A)^{n-1}$; a
codimension-free Gram-determinant formula valid for sections by subspaces of arbitrary
dimension $m\le n$, with the resulting sandwich $\lambda_{\min}^m\omega_m \le
\vol_m(\text{section}) \le \lambda_{\max}^m\omega_m$; the polar duality law
$\E(A)^\circ = \E((A^\T)^{-1})$ with the resulting Blaschke--Santal\'o equality
$\vol(\E(A))\vol(\E(A)^\circ) = \omega_n^2$ and a Santal\'o-type estimate for bodies squeezed
between ellipsoids; the theorem that the intersection body of an ellipsoid is an ellipsoid,
explicitly $I(\E(A)) = \E(\lvert\det A\rvert S^{-1})$ with $S = \sqrt{AA^\T}$, whose
restriction to unimodular positive definite generators is the involution $A \mapsto A^{-1}$
with the identity matrix as unique fixed point---so the ball is the unique unimodular
ellipsoid equal to its own intersection body; and the existence of a maximal-volume
inscribed ellipsoid in any closed bounded body containing a nondegenerate ellipsoid. Every
statement is proved from the linear-image definition by elementary spectral and
Gram-determinant arguments, requiring no integral-geometric input.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} ellipsoid, central section, Gram determinant, spectral
decomposition, polar duality, Blaschke--Santal\'o, intersection body, John ellipsoid,
convex geometry.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

The central-section behaviour of a convex body is one of the recurring themes of
high-dimensional geometry. Questions of the form ``what do the $(n-1)$-dimensional slices
through the origin tell us about the body?'' underlie the Busemann--Petty problem, the
slicing (hyperplane) conjecture, the dual Brunn--Minkowski theory, and much of the geometry
of Banach spaces. In almost all of these settings the answers are hard, partial, or
dimension-dependent.

Ellipsoids are the exception. Because an ellipsoid is by definition a linear image of a
ball, and because linear maps act on volume through a single scalar---the
determinant---all of the slicing data of an ellipsoid can be computed in closed form. This
makes ellipsoids the calibration standard of the subject: they are the extremisers in the
Blaschke--Santal\'o inequality, they are the bodies for which the Busemann--Petty problem is
trivially affirmative, and via John's theorem they are the universal approximating family
for arbitrary convex bodies.

The present work carries out that computation systematically, from a single definition, and
pushes it as far as it will go: to sections of arbitrary codimension, to the equality cases
of the resulting inequalities, to the polar dual, to the intersection body, and to the
existence of extremal inscribed ellipsoids.

\subsection{The point of view}

The organising decision is to define an ellipsoid \emph{dynamically}, as an image
\[
  \E(A) = A B^n = \{Ax : \norm{x} \le 1\},
\]
rather than \emph{statically}, as a sublevel set $\{x : \ip{Qx}{x}\le1\}$ of a positive
definite quadratic form. The two descriptions are equivalent (Proposition~\ref{prop:quad}),
and the static one is often more convenient for checking membership; but the dynamic one is
what makes every volume computation a determinant computation and every section computation
a Gram-determinant computation. The price is that the generator $A$ is not
unique---$A$ and $AU$ generate the same ellipsoid for orthogonal $U$---and a recurring
technical theme is to exploit this redundancy rather than fight it.

\subsection{Notation}

Throughout, $\R^n$ carries the standard inner product and Euclidean norm; $B^n = \{x :
\norm{x}\le1\}$ is the closed unit ball, $B(0,r)$ the closed ball of radius $r$, and
$\omega_n = \vol_n(B^n) = \pi^{n/2}/\Gamma(n/2+1)$. We write $A^\T$ for the transpose,
$\diag(d)$ for the diagonal matrix with entries $d_i$, and $I$ for the identity. A matrix
$A$ is \emph{positive definite}, $A \succ 0$, if it is symmetric with $\ip{Ax}{x} > 0$ for
$x \ne 0$, and \emph{positive semidefinite}, $A \succeq 0$, if $\ip{Ax}{x} \ge 0$. For
$A \succeq 0$, $\sqrt{A}$ is the unique positive semidefinite square root. For a unit vector
$u$, $u^\perp = \{x : \ip{x}{u} = 0\}$.

\section{Ellipsoids, volume and spectral normal form}

\subsection{Definition and basic structure}

\begin{definition}[Ellipsoid]\label{def:ell}
For an $n\times n$ real matrix $A$, the \emph{ellipsoid generated by $A$} is
\[
  \E(A) := A\,B^n = \{\, Ax : \norm{x}\le1 \,\}.
\]
\end{definition}

We call $A$ a \emph{generator}. Singular generators produce degenerate (lower-dimensional)
ellipsoids; they are deliberately admitted, and in Section~\ref{sec:john} their presence is
what makes a certain constraint set closed.

\begin{proposition}[Membership]\label{prop:mem}
If $\det A \ne 0$ then $x \in \E(A) \iff \norm{A^{-1}x} \le 1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
If $x = Ay$ with $\norm{y}\le1$ then $A^{-1}x = y$. Conversely if $\norm{A^{-1}x}\le1$ then
$x = A(A^{-1}x)$ exhibits $x$ as an image of a ball point.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Elementary properties]
For every $A$, the set $\E(A)$ is convex, compact and centrally symmetric,
$-\E(A) = \E(A)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Convexity and compactness are preserved under continuous linear images of the convex
compact ball. Symmetry: $-\E(A) = A(-B^n) = AB^n$, since $-B^n = B^n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Quadratic-form description]\label{prop:quad}
If $\det A \ne 0$ then
$\E(A) = \{\, x \in \R^n : \ip{(AA^\T)^{-1}x}{x} \le 1 \,\}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:mem}, $x \in \E(A)$ iff $\norm{A^{-1}x}^2 \le 1$, and
$\norm{A^{-1}x}^2 = \ip{(A^{-1})^\T A^{-1}x}{x}$ with
$(A^{-1})^\T A^{-1} = (AA^\T)^{-1}$.
\end{proof}

Thus the ellipsoid depends on $A$ only through the Gram matrix $AA^\T$. We record the
general identity, used throughout,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gram}
  \norm{Ay}^2 = \ip{(A^\T A)y}{y},
\end{equation}
valid for rectangular $A$ as well.

\subsection{Volume}

\begin{theorem}[Volume of an ellipsoid]\label{thm:vol}
For every $n\times n$ matrix $A$, $\ \vol(\E(A)) = \lvert\det A\rvert\,\omega_n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lebesgue measure is a Haar measure on $\R^n$ and a linear map $L$ scales it by
$\lvert\det L\rvert$. Apply this with $L : x \mapsto Ax$ and the set $B^n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Determinant normalization]
If $\det A = 1$ then $\vol(\E(A)) = \omega_n$: a unimodular generator produces an ellipsoid
of exactly the volume of the unit ball.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Orthogonal invariance and the spectral form}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:orth}
If $U^\T U = I$ then $\norm{Uy} = \norm{y}$ for all $y$; if in addition $UU^\T = I$ then
$U(B(0,r)) = B(0,r)$ for every $r \ge 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By \eqref{eq:gram}, $\norm{Uy}^2 = \ip{U^\T Uy}{y} = \norm{y}^2$. For the second claim,
$U(B(0,r)) \subseteq B(0,r)$ by isometry, and conversely $x = U(U^\T x)$ with
$\norm{U^\T x} = \norm{x}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Right orthogonal invariance]\label{thm:orthinv}
If $U$ is orthogonal then $\E(AU) = \E(A)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\E(AU) = (AU)(B^n) = A(U(B^n)) = AB^n$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:orth}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Composition]\label{thm:comp}
For all $A, B$: $\ \E(AB) = A(\E(B))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Associativity of the image: $(AB)(B^n) = A(B(B^n))$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Spectral normal form]\label{thm:spec}
Let $A \succ 0$ with eigenvalues $\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_n > 0$ and orthonormal eigenvector
matrix $U$. Then $\E(A) = U\bigl(\E(\diag(\lambda))\bigr)$. Every ellipsoid is therefore an
orthogonal image of a diagonal ellipsoid whose semiaxes are the eigenvalues of its positive
definite generator.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By the spectral theorem $A = U\diag(\lambda)U^\T$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:comp},
$\E(A) = \E\bigl((U\diag(\lambda))U^\T\bigr)$, which equals $\E(U\diag(\lambda))$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:orthinv}, and this is $U(\E(\diag(\lambda)))$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:comp}
again.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Eigenvalue sandwich]\label{thm:sandwich}
Let $A \succ 0$ have all eigenvalues in $[\ell,h]$ with $\ell \ge 0$. Then
$B(0,\ell) \subseteq \E(A) \subseteq B(0,h)$; in particular
$B(0,\lambda_{\min}) \subseteq \E(A) \subseteq B(0,\lambda_{\max})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By \eqref{eq:gram} and the spectral theorem, $\norm{Ay}^2 = \sum_i \lambda_i^2 c_i^2$ where
$c$ are the eigenbasis coordinates of $y$, so $\ell\norm{y}\le\norm{Ay}\le h\norm{y}$. The
upper inclusion follows at once. For the lower one, given $\norm{x}\le\ell$ (with $\ell>0$;
the case $\ell = 0$ is vacuous) set $y = A^{-1}x$; then $\ell\norm{y} \le \norm{Ay} =
\norm{x} \le \ell$, so $\norm{y}\le1$ and $x \in \E(A)$.
\end{proof}

Since $\det A = \prod_i\lambda_i$ for $A \succ 0$, Theorem~\ref{thm:vol} also reads
$\vol(\E(A)) = (\lambda_1\cdots\lambda_n)\omega_n$.

\section{The central-section formula}

\subsection{Sections as preimages}

\begin{definition}[Central section in a frame]\label{def:sec}
Let $\iota$ be an $n\times m$ matrix with orthonormal columns, $\iota^\T\iota = I_m$, so
that $\iota : \R^m \to V := \ran\iota$ is an isometry onto an $m$-dimensional subspace. The
\emph{central section of $\E(A)$ in the frame $\iota$} is
$\Sigma(A,\iota) := \{ y \in \R^m : \iota y \in \E(A)\}$, and
$\vol_m(\Sigma(A,\iota)) = \vol_m(\E(A)\cap V)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:secpre}
If $\det A \ne 0$ then $\Sigma(A,\iota) = \{ y : \norm{A^{-1}\iota y} \le 1\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Proposition~\ref{prop:mem}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The Gram-determinant volume formula}

\begin{lemma}[Injectivity criterion]\label{lem:inj}
If the $n\times m$ matrix $T$ has trivial kernel then $T^\T T \succ 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$T^\T T$ is symmetric and $\ip{T^\T Ty}{y} = \norm{Ty}^2 \ge 0$ by \eqref{eq:gram}, with
equality iff $y = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Gram-determinant volume formula]\label{thm:gramvol}
Let $T$ be an $n\times m$ matrix with $T^\T T \succ 0$. Then
\[
  \vol_m\{\, y \in \R^m : \norm{Ty} \le 1 \,\} = \frac{\omega_m}{\sqrt{\det(T^\T T)}}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By \eqref{eq:gram} the set is $\{y : \ip{Gy}{y}\le1\}$ with $G = T^\T T \succ 0$. Write
$G = R^\T R$ with $R = \sqrt{G} \succ 0$; the set is then $\{y : \norm{Ry}\le1\} =
R^{-1}(B^m)$, of volume $\lvert\det R^{-1}\rvert\omega_m = \omega_m/\sqrt{\det G}$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:vol}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Codimension-free section formula]\label{thm:codimfree}
Let $\det A \ne 0$ and let $\iota$ be an $n\times m$ frame with $\iota^\T\iota = I_m$. Then
$(A^{-1}\iota)^\T(A^{-1}\iota) \succ 0$ and
\[
  \vol_m\bigl(\E(A)\cap\ran\iota\bigr)
   = \frac{\omega_m}{\sqrt{\det\bigl((A^{-1}\iota)^\T(A^{-1}\iota)\bigr)}} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $(A^{-1}\iota)y = 0$ then $\iota y = 0$ (apply $A$), hence $y = \iota^\T\iota y = 0$; so
$A^{-1}\iota$ is injective and Lemma~\ref{lem:inj} applies. Combine
Lemma~\ref{lem:secpre} with Theorem~\ref{thm:gramvol}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The hyperplane Gram identity}

\begin{theorem}[Central-section Gram identity]\label{thm:gramid}
Let $n = m+1$, $\det A \ne 0$, $\norm{u} = 1$, and let $\iota$ be an $n\times m$ matrix with
\[
  \iota^\T\iota = I_m, \qquad \iota\,\iota^\T = I_n - uu^\T
\]
(an orthonormal frame of the hyperplane $u^\perp$). Then
\[
  \det\bigl((A^{-1}\iota)^\T(A^{-1}\iota)\bigr) = \frac{\norm{A^\T u}^2}{(\det A)^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Set $T = A^{-1}\iota$ and $p = (A^\T u)/\norm{A^\T u}^2$ (note $A^\T u \ne 0$), and form the
augmented square matrix $N = [\,T \mid p\,]$.

\emph{(i) The augmented Gram matrix is block diagonal.} Its leading $m\times m$ block is
$T^\T T$; the off-diagonal block is $T^\T p = \iota^\T u/\norm{A^\T u}^2 = 0$, because
$\iota\,\iota^\T u = (I - uu^\T)u = 0$ and multiplication on the left by $\iota^\T$ with
$\iota^\T\iota = I_m$ gives $\iota^\T u = 0$. The last diagonal entry is
$\norm{p}^2 = 1/\norm{A^\T u}^2$. Hence
$\det(N^\T N) = \det(T^\T T)\,/\,\norm{A^\T u}^2$.

\emph{(ii) The augmented determinant is computable.} We have $AN = [\,\iota\mid w\,]$ with
$w = AA^\T u/\norm{A^\T u}^2$ and $\ip{w}{u} = 1$. Since $[\,\iota\mid u\,]$ is orthogonal
(its Gram matrix is the identity, by the two frame conditions), expanding along the last
column gives $\det[\,\iota\mid w\,] = \pm\ip{u}{w} = \pm1$. So $\lvert\det(AN)\rvert = 1$,
whence $\lvert\det N\rvert = 1/\lvert\det A\rvert$ and $\det(N^\T N) = 1/(\det A)^2$.

Comparing (i) and (ii) yields the identity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Central section formula]\label{thm:section}
Let $\det A \ne 0$, let $u$ be a unit vector in $\R^n$, and let $\iota$ be an orthonormal
frame of $u^\perp$ as above. Then
\[
  \boxed{\ \vol_{n-1}\bigl(\E(A)\cap u^\perp\bigr)
    = \frac{\lvert\det A\rvert}{\norm{A^\T u}}\;\omega_{n-1}. \ }
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Substitute Theorem~\ref{thm:gramid} into Theorem~\ref{thm:codimfree} with $m = n-1$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Frames exist, and the answer does not depend on them}

\begin{theorem}[Frame independence]
If $\iota$ and $\kappa$ are two orthonormal frames of the same hyperplane $u^\perp$, then
$\vol_{n-1}(\Sigma(A,\iota)) = \vol_{n-1}(\Sigma(A,\kappa))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both are computed by Theorem~\ref{thm:section} to be
$\lvert\det A\rvert\,\omega_{n-1}/\norm{A^\T u}$, an expression not involving the frame.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence of slicing frames]\label{thm:frames}
Every unit vector $u \in \R^{n}$, $n = m+1 \ge 1$, admits an $n\times m$ matrix $\iota$ with
$\iota^\T\iota = I_m$ and $\iota\,\iota^\T = I_n - uu^\T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Extend $u$ to an orthonormal basis $(u, v_1,\dots,v_m)$ and let $Q$ be the orthogonal matrix
with these columns; then $QQ^\T = I$ reads $uu^\T + \sum_j v_jv_j^\T = I$. Take
$\iota = [\,v_1\mid\cdots\mid v_m\,]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Unconditional section formula]\label{cor:uncond}
For every invertible $A$ the function
$u \mapsto \vol_{n-1}(\E(A)\cap u^\perp)$ is well defined on the unit sphere and equals
$\lvert\det A\rvert\,\omega_{n-1}/\norm{A^\T u}$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Spectral slicing bounds and their equality cases}

Throughout this section $A \succ 0$ with eigenvalues $\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_n>0$; then
$A^\T = A$, $\det A > 0$, and the section formula reads
$\vol_{n-1}(\E(A)\cap u^\perp) = (\det A/\norm{Au})\,\omega_{n-1}$.

\begin{lemma}[Weighted average]\label{lem:avg}
Let $A \succ 0$ and let $u$ be a unit vector with coordinates $c$ in an orthonormal
eigenbasis of $A$. Then $\norm{Au}^2 = \sum_i \lambda_i^2c_i^2$ with $\sum_i c_i^2 = 1$;
consequently, if $0 \le \ell \le \lambda_i \le h$ for all $i$, then
$\ell \le \norm{Au} \le h$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$u = \sum_i c_iw_i$ with $\sum c_i^2 = 1$ and $Au = \sum_i\lambda_ic_iw_i$; the resulting
sum is a convex combination of the $\lambda_i^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Spectral slicing bounds]\label{thm:slice}
Let $A\succ0$ with $0 < \ell \le \lambda_i \le h$ for all $i$, and $\norm{u} = 1$. Then
\[
  \frac{\det A}{h}\,\omega_{n-1} \;\le\; \vol_{n-1}\bigl(\E(A)\cap u^\perp\bigr)
    \;\le\; \frac{\det A}{\ell}\,\omega_{n-1}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Corollary~\ref{cor:uncond} with Lemma~\ref{lem:avg}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sections in eigendirections]\label{thm:eigsec}
If $Au = \lambda u$ with $\norm{u}=1$ and $\lambda > 0$ then
$\vol_{n-1}(\E(A)\cap u^\perp) = (\det A/\lambda)\,\omega_{n-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\norm{Au} = \lambda$; substitute into Corollary~\ref{cor:uncond}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Equality in a weighted average]\label{lem:eqcase}
Let $\lambda_i > 0$ with $\lambda_i \le h$, and let $\sum_i c_i^2 = 1$ and
$\sum_i \lambda_i^2c_i^2 = h^2$. Then $\lambda_ic_i = hc_i$ for every $i$; equivalently
$c_i = 0$ whenever $\lambda_i < h$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\sum_i (h^2-\lambda_i^2)c_i^2 = 0$ is a sum of nonnegative terms, so each vanishes.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Extremal norms are eigenvalue equations]\label{thm:normeq}
Let $A\succ0$ and $\norm{u} = 1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $\lambda_i \le h$ for all $i$, then $\norm{Au} = h \iff Au = hu$.
\item If $\lambda_i \ge \ell \ge 0$ for all $i$, then $\norm{Au} = \ell \iff Au = \ell u$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) is the computation of Theorem~\ref{thm:eigsec}. ($\Rightarrow$) for (1):
Lemma~\ref{lem:avg} gives $\sum\lambda_i^2c_i^2 = h^2$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:eqcase} gives
$\lambda_ic_i = hc_i$ for all $i$, i.e.\ $Au = hu$. Part (2) is the same argument with
$\sum_i(\lambda_i^2-\ell^2)c_i^2 = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Extremal sections are exactly the eigendirections]\label{thm:rigid}
Let $A \succ 0$ and $\norm{u} = 1$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $\ell > 0$ is a lower bound for the spectrum, then
  $\vol_{n-1}(\E(A)\cap u^\perp) = (\det A/\ell)\,\omega_{n-1} \iff Au = \ell u$.
\item If $h > 0$ is an upper bound for the spectrum, then
  $\vol_{n-1}(\E(A)\cap u^\perp) = (\det A/h)\,\omega_{n-1} \iff Au = hu$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The section volume is $(\det A/\norm{Au})\omega_{n-1}$ with $\omega_{n-1} > 0$, $\det A>0$
and $\norm{Au}>0$, so the volume equality is equivalent to $\norm{Au} = \ell$ (resp.\ $h$).
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:normeq}.
\end{proof}

Thus the maximal-volume central sections are exactly those orthogonal to a
minimal-eigenvalue eigenvector, and the minimal-volume ones exactly those orthogonal to a
maximal-eigenvalue eigenvector: the section-volume function on the sphere determines
$\lambda_{\min}$, $\lambda_{\max}$ and the corresponding eigenspaces.

\begin{theorem}[Determinant-normalization identity]\label{thm:prod}
For $A \succ 0$ in $\R^n$, $\ \prod_{i=1}^n \dfrac{\det A}{\lambda_i} = (\det A)^{\,n-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\prod_i(\det A/\lambda_i) = (\det A)^n/\prod_i\lambda_i = (\det A)^{n-1}$.
\end{proof}

This is a conservation law: the product of the $n$ normalised principal section volumes is
pinned by the volume, so one cannot make all principal sections small.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:unimod}
If $A \succ 0$ and $\det A = 1$, then some eigenvalue is $\le 1$ and some is $\ge 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Otherwise $\prod_i\lambda_i > 1$ or $< 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Extremal sections of a unimodular ellipsoid]\label{thm:straddle}
Let $A\succ0$ with $\det A = 1$. Then there are unit vectors $u_+$, $u_-$ with
\[
  \vol_{n-1}\bigl(\E(A)\cap u_+^\perp\bigr) \;\ge\; \omega_{n-1} \;\ge\;
  \vol_{n-1}\bigl(\E(A)\cap u_-^\perp\bigr),
\]
both eigenvectors, for an eigenvalue $\le 1$ and $\ge 1$ respectively.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Lemma~\ref{lem:unimod} with Theorem~\ref{thm:eigsec}.
\end{proof}

\section{Sections of arbitrary codimension}

\begin{theorem}[Determinant bounds from quadratic-form bounds]\label{thm:detbound}
Let $G \succ 0$ be $m\times m$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $\ip{Gy}{y} \le c\norm{y}^2$ for all $y$, then $\det G \le c^{\,m}$.
\item If $c \ge 0$ and $c\norm{y}^2 \le \ip{Gy}{y}$ for all $y$, then $c^{\,m}\le\det G$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Test on unit eigenvectors to bound each eigenvalue $\mu_j$ of $G$, then multiply the $m$
inequalities, all sides being nonnegative, and use $\det G = \prod_j \mu_j$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Codimension-free slicing sandwich]\label{thm:msandwich}
Let $A\succ0$ in $\R^n$ have all eigenvalues in $[\ell,h]$ with $0<\ell\le h$, and let
$\iota$ be any $n\times m$ frame with $\iota^\T\iota = I_m$. Then
\[
  \ell^{\,m}\,\omega_m \;\le\; \vol_m\bigl(\E(A)\cap\ran\iota\bigr) \;\le\; h^{\,m}\,\omega_m .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $G = (A^{-1}\iota)^\T(A^{-1}\iota)$. For $y \in \R^m$ put $z = \iota y$ (so
$\norm{z} = \norm{y}$) and $w = A^{-1}z$; by \eqref{eq:gram}, $\ip{Gy}{y} = \norm{w}^2$. By
Lemma~\ref{lem:avg} applied to $w$, $\ell\norm{w}\le\norm{Aw} = \norm{z} = \norm{y} \le
h\norm{w}$, hence $h^{-2}\norm{y}^2 \le \ip{Gy}{y} \le \ell^{-2}\norm{y}^2$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:detbound}, $h^{-2m}\le\det G\le\ell^{-2m}$, so
$\ell^m \le (\det G)^{-1/2} \le h^m$; conclude with Theorem~\ref{thm:codimfree}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Coordinate sections of a diagonal ellipsoid]\label{thm:coord}
Let $d_1,\dots,d_n$ be nonzero, $A = \diag(d)$, and let $f : \{1,\dots,m\}\to\{1,\dots,n\}$
be injective with $\iota$ the corresponding coordinate frame. Then
\[
  \vol_m\bigl(\E(A)\cap\operatorname{span}\{e_{f(1)},\dots,e_{f(m)}\}\bigr)
    = \Bigl(\prod_{j=1}^m \lvert d_{f(j)}\rvert\Bigr)\,\omega_m .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$A^{-1}\iota$ has columns $d_{f(j)}^{-1}e_{f(j)}$, so the Gram matrix is
$\diag(d_{f(j)}^{-2})$ with determinant $\prod_j d_{f(j)}^{-2}$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:codimfree}.
\end{proof}

For $n=2$, $A = \diag(a,b)$ and the frame $e_1$, the section is the segment of $1$-volume
$2a$; for $n=3$, $A = \diag(a,b,c)$ and the frame $(e_1,e_2)$, the section is the ellipse of
area $\pi ab$.

\section{Polar duality}

\begin{definition}[Polar set]
For $S \subseteq \R^n$, $\ S^\circ := \{ y \in \R^n : \ip{y}{x}\le1 \text{ for all } x\in S\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Calculus of polars]\label{prop:polarcalc}
For all $S,T$: (i) $S\subseteq T \Rightarrow T^\circ \subseteq S^\circ$;
(ii) $(S\cup T)^\circ = S^\circ\cap T^\circ$; (iii) $(B^n)^\circ = B^n$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
(i), (ii) are immediate. (iii) Cauchy--Schwarz gives $B^n \subseteq (B^n)^\circ$; conversely
testing at $x = y/\norm{y}$ gives $\norm{y}\le1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Linear covariance]\label{thm:cov}
For invertible $A$ and any set $S$, $\ (A\,S)^\circ = (A^\T)^{-1}S^\circ$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$y \in (AS)^\circ$ iff $\ip{A^\T y}{x}\le1$ for all $x \in S$ iff $A^\T y \in S^\circ$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Polar of an ellipsoid]\label{cor:polarell}
For invertible $A$, $\ \E(A)^\circ = \E\bigl((A^\T)^{-1}\bigr)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$\E(A)^\circ = (AB^n)^\circ = (A^\T)^{-1}(B^n)^\circ = (A^\T)^{-1}B^n$, by
Theorem~\ref{thm:cov} and Proposition~\ref{prop:polarcalc}(iii).
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Bipolar theorem for ellipsoids]
For invertible $A$, $\ (\E(A)^\circ)^\circ = \E(A)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply Corollary~\ref{cor:polarell} twice; the inverse transpose of $(A^\T)^{-1}$ is $A$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Volume of the polar]\label{cor:polarvol}
For invertible $A$, $\ \vol(\E(A)^\circ) = \lvert\det A\rvert^{-1}\omega_n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Blaschke--Santal\'o equality for ellipsoids]\label{thm:santalo}
For every invertible $A$, $\ \vol(\E(A))\cdot\vol(\E(A)^\circ) = \omega_n^2$: every
ellipsoid has exactly the volume product of the ball.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Multiply Theorem~\ref{thm:vol} and Corollary~\ref{cor:polarvol}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Linear invariance of the volume product]
For invertible $A$ and measurable $S$,
$\ \vol(AS)\cdot\vol((AS)^\circ) = \vol(S)\cdot\vol(S^\circ)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:cov} the polar is $(A^\T)^{-1}S^\circ$; the factors
$\lvert\det A\rvert$ and $\lvert\det A\rvert^{-1}$ cancel.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Santal\'o-type estimate for squeezed bodies]\label{thm:squeeze}
If $\E(A)\subseteq S\subseteq \E(B)$ with $A$ invertible, then
\[
  \vol(S)\cdot\vol(S^\circ) \;\le\; \frac{\lvert\det B\rvert}{\lvert\det A\rvert}\;\omega_n^2 .
\]
Taking $A = B$ recovers Theorem~\ref{thm:santalo} exactly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Monotonicity gives $\vol(S)\le\lvert\det B\rvert\omega_n$; antitonicity of polarity gives
$S^\circ\subseteq\E(A)^\circ$, so $\vol(S^\circ)\le\lvert\det A\rvert^{-1}\omega_n$.
Multiply.
\end{proof}

\section{The intersection body of an ellipsoid}

For a star body $K$, the \emph{intersection body} $IK$ is the star body whose radial
function is $\rho_{IK}(u) = \vol_{n-1}(K\cap u^\perp)/\omega_{n-1}$. Intersection bodies are
the central objects of the dual Brunn--Minkowski theory and the key to the Busemann--Petty
problem. By Corollary~\ref{cor:uncond}, for an ellipsoid this function is
$u \mapsto \lvert\det A\rvert/\norm{A^\T u}$.

\begin{definition}[Gram square root and intersection generator]
For invertible $A$ put $S := \sqrt{AA^\T} \succ 0$ and
$\Ical(A) := \lvert\det A\rvert\,S^{-1}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sqrt}
For invertible $A$: $AA^\T \succ 0$, $\det S = \lvert\det A\rvert$, and
$\Ical(A)^{-1} = S/\lvert\det A\rvert$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\ip{AA^\T x}{x} = \norm{A^\T x}^2 > 0$ for $x\ne0$; then
$(\det S)^2 = \det(AA^\T) = (\det A)^2$ with $\det S > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Radial description]\label{lem:radial}
For invertible $H$, unit $u$ and $t\ge0$: $\ tu \in \E(H) \iff t\norm{H^{-1}u}\le1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[The intersection body of an ellipsoid is an ellipsoid]\label{thm:ib}
Let $A$ be invertible, $\norm{u} = 1$, $t \ge 0$. Then
\[
  t u \in \E(\Ical(A)) \iff t\,\norm{A^\T u} \le \lvert\det A\rvert
   \iff t \le \frac{\vol_{n-1}(\E(A)\cap u^\perp)}{\omega_{n-1}} .
\]
Hence $I(\E(A)) = \E\bigl(\lvert\det A\rvert\,(\sqrt{AA^\T})^{-1}\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:radial} and Lemma~\ref{lem:sqrt}, $tu \in \E(\Ical(A))$ iff
$t\norm{Su}\le\lvert\det A\rvert$. Since $S$ is symmetric with $S^2 = AA^\T$, we get
$\norm{Su}^2 = \ip{AA^\T u}{u} = \norm{A^\T u}^2$, so $\norm{Su} = \norm{A^\T u}$. The
second equivalence is Corollary~\ref{cor:uncond} rearranged.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Determinant and volume of the intersection body]
For invertible $A$ in $\R^n$: $\ \det\Ical(A) = \lvert\det A\rvert^{\,n-1}$ and
$\vol(I(\E(A))) = \lvert\det A\rvert^{\,n-1}\omega_n$. In particular a unimodular ellipsoid
has an intersection body of exactly the volume of the ball.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\det(\lvert\det A\rvert S^{-1}) = \lvert\det A\rvert^n/\det S = \lvert\det A\rvert^{n-1}$
by Lemma~\ref{lem:sqrt}; then apply Theorem~\ref{thm:vol}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:ibpd}
If $A \succ 0$ then $\Ical(A) = (\det A)\,A^{-1}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$AA^\T = A^2$, so $S = \sqrt{A^2} = A$ by uniqueness of the positive semidefinite square
root, and $\det A > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Involution on unimodular generators]\label{thm:invol}
If $A\succ0$ and $\det A = 1$ then $\Ical(A) = A^{-1}$ and $\Ical(\Ical(A)) = A$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Proposition~\ref{prop:ibpd} twice; $A^{-1}$ is again positive definite of determinant $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The ball is the unique unimodular fixed point]\label{thm:fix}
If $A\succ0$, $\det A = 1$ and $\Ical(A) = A$, then $A = I$; equivalently $\E(A) = B^n$.
Thus the ball is the unique unimodular ellipsoid equal to its own intersection body.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:invol} the hypothesis reads $A^{-1} = A$, i.e.\ $A^2 = I$. A positive
definite matrix with $A^2 = I$ has all eigenvalues positive and squaring to $1$, hence equal
to $1$, so $A = I$ by the spectral theorem.
\end{proof}

\section{Existence of the maximal-volume inscribed ellipsoid}\label{sec:john}

\begin{definition}[Inscribed generators]
For $K \subseteq \R^n$, $\ \mathcal{G}(K) := \{\, A : A \succeq 0,\ \E(A)\subseteq K \,\}$.
\end{definition}

Admitting degenerate (singular positive semidefinite) generators is essential: the flat
limits of nondegenerate inscribed ellipsoids are exactly what a closed constraint set must
contain.

\begin{lemma}[Closedness]\label{lem:closed}
If $K$ is closed then $\mathcal{G}(K)$ is closed.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The positive semidefinite cone is closed; and $\{A : \E(A)\subseteq K\} =
\bigcap_{x\in B^n}\{A : Ax \in K\}$ is an intersection of preimages of the closed set $K$
under the continuous maps $A \mapsto Ax$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Boundedness]\label{lem:bdd}
If $K \subseteq B(0,R)$ then every $A \in \mathcal{G}(K)$ has all entries bounded by $R$;
hence $\mathcal{G}(K)$ is compact.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$Ae_j \in K \subseteq B(0,R)$ bounds the $j$-th column, and each coordinate of a vector is
bounded by its norm.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Existence of a maximiser]\label{thm:john}
Let $K$ be closed with $K \subseteq B(0,R)$ and suppose $\E(A_0)\subseteq K$ for some
$A_0 \succ 0$. Then there exists $A \succ 0$ with $\E(A) \subseteq K$ and
$\det A \ge \det B$, equivalently $\vol(\E(A)) \ge \vol(\E(B))$, for every $B \succeq 0$
with $\E(B)\subseteq K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\mathcal{G}(K)$ is nonempty and compact by Lemmas~\ref{lem:closed}--\ref{lem:bdd}, and
$\det$ is continuous, hence attains a maximum at some $A \in \mathcal{G}(K)$. Since
$\det A \ge \det A_0 > 0$, the maximiser is nonsingular, hence positive definite. The volume
form of the statement is Theorem~\ref{thm:vol}.
\end{proof}

The maximiser is the \emph{John ellipsoid} of $K$; uniqueness follows from strict concavity
of $\det^{1/n}$ on positive definite matrices, and combined with
Theorem~\ref{thm:squeeze} it yields the classical volume-product estimates for symmetric
convex bodies.

\section{Algorithms}

All the results above are constructive.

\paragraph{Exact section volume.} Given invertible $A$ and unit $u$: compute
$\delta = \lvert\det A\rvert$ ($O(n^3)$ by LU), $\nu = \norm{A^\T u}$ ($O(n^2)$), return
$(\delta/\nu)\omega_{n-1}$ with $\omega_m = \pi^{m/2}/\Gamma(m/2+1)$. Once $\delta$ is
cached each further direction costs $O(n^2)$.

\paragraph{Section volume of any codimension.} Solve $AT = \iota$, form $G = T^\T T$, return
$\omega_m/\sqrt{\det G}$; total $O(n^3 + nm^2 + m^3)$. For $m = n-1$ agreement with the
previous procedure is a sharp numerical test of Theorem~\ref{thm:gramid}.

\paragraph{Extremal directions.} By Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid}, diagonalise $A$ ($O(n^3)$) and
read off the eigenvector for $\lambda_{\min}$ (maximal section) and for $\lambda_{\max}$
(minimal section). No optimisation over the sphere is required.

\paragraph{Slicing frames.} By Theorem~\ref{thm:frames}: take the $Q$ factor of a QR
decomposition of a matrix whose first column is $u$, and drop the first column; verify
$\iota^\T\iota = I$ and $\iota\iota^\T = I - uu^\T$ to machine precision.

\paragraph{Intersection body.} Diagonalise $AA^\T$, replace eigenvalues by square roots to
get $S$, and set $\Ical(A) = \lvert\det A\rvert S^{-1}$; compare its radial function with the
computed section volumes.

\paragraph{Monte-Carlo cross-validation.} Sample $y$ uniformly in a cube containing the
pulled-back section, test $\norm{A^{-1}\iota y}\le1$, and scale the acceptance rate by the
cube volume; convergence is $O(N^{-1/2})$.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What makes it all work}

Three mechanisms account for every theorem above. \emph{Determinant homogeneity}: volume
under a linear map scales by $\lvert\det\rvert$, which alone gives the volume formula and,
through cancellation, the linear invariance of the volume product and the
Blaschke--Santal\'o equality. \emph{The Gram determinant}: for rectangular maps the
determinant is replaced by $\sqrt{\det T^\T T}$, and all section formulas are evaluations of
this one quantity, the hyperplane case collapsing to a single vector norm because appending
one orthogonal column makes the matrix square. \emph{The spectral theorem}: positive
definite generators are diagonal in an orthonormal basis, turning norms into weighted
averages of eigenvalues, so inequalities come from bounding the average and equality cases
from the vanishing of a sum of nonnegative terms.

The redundancy $\E(AU) = \E(A)$ for orthogonal $U$ is a tool rather than a defect: it lets
one replace an arbitrary generator by the positive definite $\sqrt{AA^\T}$ whenever spectral
reasoning is needed, which is precisely the manoeuvre behind Theorem~\ref{thm:ib}.

\subsection{Relation to the classical theory}

The Blaschke--Santal\'o inequality $\vol(K)\vol(K^\circ)\le\omega_n^2$ for symmetric convex
$K$ has ellipsoids as its equality case, and Theorem~\ref{thm:santalo} is that equality
case, obtained here for free from covariance; Theorem~\ref{thm:squeeze} converts an
ellipsoidal sandwich into a quantitative bound for general bodies. The Busemann--Petty
problem---whether uniformly smaller central sections force smaller volume, true for
$n\le4$ and false for $n\ge5$---turns on intersection bodies, and Theorem~\ref{thm:ib} shows
the ellipsoid class is closed under that operator, which is why ellipsoid-versus-ball
comparisons reduce to the explicit formula of Corollary~\ref{cor:uncond}.
Theorem~\ref{thm:john} is the existence half of John's theorem, formulated so that
degenerate generators keep the feasible set closed. Finally, Theorem~\ref{thm:straddle} is
the ellipsoidal instance of the hyperplane conjecture: a unimodular ellipsoid always has a
central section of volume at least $\omega_{n-1}$.

\subsection{Limits and future directions}

The theory here is exact but confined to origin-centred ellipsoids. Natural continuations
are: uniqueness of the John ellipsoid via strict log-concavity of the determinant together
with the contact-point characterisation; non-central and affine sections, where the section
of $\E(A)$ by $\{\ip{x}{u} = c\}$ is again an ellipsoid scaled by
$\sqrt{1 - c^2/\norm{A^\T u}^2}$, and the resulting concavity of the section function
(an ellipsoidal Brunn theorem); projection bodies and the dual theory, replacing
$\norm{A^\T u}$ by $\norm{Au}$; a complete formula-level treatment of Busemann--Petty
comparisons inside the ellipsoid class; quantitative stability versions of the rigidity
results (Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid}) and of the uniqueness of the ball
(Theorem~\ref{thm:fix}); $L_p$ and Orlicz analogues, where determinant homogeneity survives
but spectral rigidity generally does not; and the distribution of
$\vol_{n-1}(\E(A)\cap u^\perp) = \lvert\det A\rvert\,\omega_{n-1}/\norm{A^\T u}$ for random
$u$, whose concentration is the ellipsoidal shadow of the thin-shell phenomenon.

\section{Conclusion}

Defining an ellipsoid as a linear image of a ball turns every question about its volume into
a determinant computation and every question about its sections into a Gram-determinant
computation. The result is a compact and complete calculus: one scalar $\lvert\det A\rvert$
for volume, one scalar $\norm{A^\T u}$ per direction for sections, the spectrum of the
generator for the extremes, exact rigidity in the equality cases, exact self-duality of the
volume product, and a closed class under the intersection-body operator with the ball as its
unique unimodular fixed point. These identities hold in every dimension for every ellipsoid,
and they are the reason ellipsoids remain the yardstick of high-dimensional convex geometry.

